 2. 🛰️ Prepare for space war 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Maura Losch/Axios Three U.S. military leaders in just as many days changed how the public talks about war in space, including the secretive weapons already among the stars, Axios' Colin Demarest reports. 

 Last week, and in quick succession: Air Force Secretary Troy Meink said America has "on-orbit space control weapons" — a historic acknowledgment. Chief of Space Operations Gen. Douglas Schiess told reporters that "it was time" to have such a discussion and be clear-eyed about Russian and Chinese capabilities, including "being able to grapple a satellite and move it to a different orbit." Joint Chiefs Chairman Gen. Dan Caine said troops must be "prepared to fight, endure and win in global, contested environments, from the seabed to cislunar space." The big picture: Space fights will happen. They'll look nothing like conflict on the ground or at sea. And the consequences will be enormous.

